# PS4 (ps4japa) character table
#
# Renderer at $280564: byte < $80 -> tile $680 + byte, $80-$EF -> tile $740 +
# byte, $F0/$F1 -> dakuten / handakuten (combining marks), >= $FE -> end.
#
#   $00      space
#   $01-$2E  hiragana A..N (complete)
#   $2F-$31  small ya/yu/yo
#   $32-$4D  katakana A..FU   (contiguous - verified against known words)
#   $4E-$4F  HO, MA   (katakana HE absent - see note)
#   $50-$56  MI MU ME MO YA YU YO
#   $57-$5D  RA RI RU RE RO WA N   (katakana WO is absent from this bank)
#   $5E-$65  small a/i/e/o/tsu/ya/yu/yo (no small U)
#   $80-$99  A-Z      $9A-$A3 digits    $A4-$AD digits (fullwidth, 2nd bank)
#   $AE-$AF  standalone dakuten / handakuten marks
#   $B0 long vowel  $B1 !  $B2 ?  $B3 :  $B4 .  $B5 ,
#
# The tail was derived from the ROM, not from the font image: reading 8x8 kana
# off a screenshot proved unreliable three times. Anchors used:
#   $57 = RA, $5B = RO   confirmed from known item names
#   $5D  n=153, highly diverse predecessors            -> N
#   $5E  5/5 preceded by FU -> small A;  $61 -> small O (FOIE)
#
# $50-$56 hold seven slots for eight possible glyphs, so one is absent. Loan
# words resolve it: CHITANIUMU (titanium) fixes $51 = MU, SERAMIKKU (ceramic)
# fixes $50 = MI, and BUUMERAN (boomerang) plus MEIRU (mail) fix $52 = ME.
# The katakana bank omits HE, not HO. Katakana HE and hiragana HE are visually
# identical at 8x8, so the font ships only one tile ($1D) and the game spells
# katakana HE with it - HERUMETTO (helmet) is stored with the hiragana code.
# This is why $4E carries a dakuten mark in 13 of its 14 occurrences: it is HO,
# and the voiced form BO is common. $4F = MA is confirmed by AAMAA (armor) and
# MANTO (mantle), and by $4F sitting between two long-vowel marks 9x/13x.
# $53 occurs once and $55/$56 never occur, so MO/YA/YU/YO are placed by
# sequence and are the least-attested entries in this table.

# Rest of row 27, read off the VRAM font:
#   $66      left arrow
#   $67-$68  empty / grey filled square
#   $69-$75  window-frame pieces. $6B-$72 are the dakuten and handakuten marks
#            pre-composited onto the window background in four palettes - these
#            are the tiles the renderer reaches via `move.w #$6EB,d2` plus
#            `d0 >> 12`, which selects one of the eight variants.
#   $76-$77  middle dot, slash
#   $78-$7A  H, P, T in a BOLD face - not duplicates of $87/$8F/$93. They
#            fill the HP and TP labels on the character status screen and are
#            visually heavier than the thin letters in the $80 bank.
#   $7B-$7D  NOT characters. Three narrow tiles that together render the four
#            characters "o- long-vowel RE N", packing a 4-character name into
#            3 tiles so it fits the name-entry field. They only make sense as
#            a fixed run and must never be treated as individual glyphs.
#   $7E      small TSU (hiragana)
#   $7F      blank
#
# $78-$7D are left as escapes, for two different reasons.
#   $78-$7A are a distinct bold face, so mapping them to plain H/P/T would
#   conflate two different glyphs and re-encode to the thin variant.
#   $7B-$7D are the packed-name tiles above and are meaningless individually.
# The ROM bears the distinction out: the bold forms appear 0 times in either
# confirmed text region, while the thin forms appear 83 times in the dialogue
# region. The bold set is driven by the status-screen code, not the message
# renderer at $280564.
#
# Why small TSU sits so far from the other kana: row 26 ($00-$3F) is exactly
# full - 1 space + 46 hiragana + 3 small (ya/yu/yo) + 14 katakana = 64 of 64.
# There was no slack to insert it into the hiragana block without pushing the
# whole katakana bank across the row boundary, so it was appended to the tail
# of row 27. The katakana bank has its own small tsu at $62 because its small
# kana group was allocated eight slots; the hiragana group was allocated three.
#
#   $FC  106 occurrences, behaves as a line break
#   $FB  2 occurrences, control code
#   $67  2 occurrences
#
# SCOPE: this table is the 8x8 bank only. It covers item / monster / character
# names, battle text, the status menu and the item-collection descriptions.
# The story dialogue uses a completely separate 16x16 system whose glyphs are
# composed into VRAM per message (four 8x8 tiles per character), so it needs
# kanji and cannot use this one-byte encoding. None of it is handled here.

*FE=END
*F0=dakuten
*F1=handakuten

